Note = {40th International Seminar on Nuclear War and Planetary Emergencies,
   Erice, ITALY, AUG 19-24, 2008},
Abstract = {An increase in forestation, good forest management and high forestry
   stocks are significant for the long-term, sequestration of carbon in the
   forest. In accordance with the Kyoto protocol (1997), Lithuania
   committed to reduce emission of green house gasses (GHG) in the
   atmosphere by 8\% and effectively implement the programme of mitigating
   the consequences of climate change.
   The aim of this study uses analysis of statistical data on Land Use and
   Land Use Changes (LULUCH), growth of forests and applying common
   accepted methodology to reveal the impact of forestation and forestry on
   CO2 sequestration and its balance in Lithuania as territorial ecosystem
   in historical perspective.
   The data presented in the article are showing that the growth of fuel
   and energy consumption in the post-war Lithuania was followed by
   exponential GHG emission and reached its maxima 22 Mt equivalent fuel
   (eJ.) and 48.1 Mt CO2 in 1990. Since that time, the structure of primary
   energy sources used has been considerably changed: the part of nuclear
   energy increased up to 38\%, fuel wood consumption augmented 4 times
   also total fuel consumption decrease twice due to the decline
   (1990-1995) and structural changes in the national economy. From the
   other hand over the period 1948-1993 due to an increase in forest cover
   from 19.7 to 30.1\%, restriction of annual felling (to 30-40\% of annual
   gross increment), application of selective cuttings, special thinning
   which allowed to reach the maximum stands increment, also due to broad
   scale of forests land reclamation and protective measures the quantity
   of the phytomass per ha augmented twice whereas the total carbon pool in
   forests tripled. CO2 sequestration in forests have been increased its
   balance in territorial ecosystem improved. This allowed Lithuania hawing
   7-9\% of annual growth of GDP to fulfil Kyoto requirements: till Kyoto
   (19781990) each year 20-25\% and after Kyoto (1991-2007) 50-55\% emitted
   CO2 are accumulated by the gross mean additional increment in forests
   and stored in logs.
   In the article, fuel consumption GHG emission LUCH in Lithuania for the
   last several decades also the dynamics of annual growing stock, gross
   increment, removal, changes of phytomass above and below ground are
   given.},
