   methods TO-14A, LP-IA, TO-15 and canister technology},
Booktitle = {VOLATILE ORGANIC COMPOUNDS IN THE ENVIRONMENT - RISK ASSESSMET AND
   NEUROTOXICITY},
Series = {ADVANCES IN OCCUPATIONAL MEDICINE \& REHABILITATION},
Year = {1997},
Volume = {3},
Number = {3},
Pages = {109-114},
Note = {International Conference on Volatile Organic Compounds, SALVATORE
   MAUGERI FDN MED CTR, PAVIA, ITALY, OCT 05-07, 1997},
Organization = {Univ Pavia; Salvatore Maugeri Fdn; Int Soc Built Environm; Natl Res
   Council, Rome; AgipPetroli; BASF; CON TEC Engn; DANI; Draeger Italiana;
   FIAT Auto; FKV; Instrumentat Lab; IPSOA Editore Indicitalia; Perkin
   Elmer; Pollut Srl Clean Air Syst; San Air Ambiente; Tecnol Ind \&
   Ambientali; Unione Petrolifera; Zambelli},
Abstract = {The American Clean Air Act Amendments recognised VOCs as co-responsible
   for some diseases including respiratory system tumours and other
   irreversible pathologies. As a consequence it was decided that
   monitoring of VOCs was necessary in all areas where there was rapid
   urban or industrial development and VOCs could reach alarming levels. In
   the past, analytes present in air samples at tenths of ppm level were
   just ignored because they were too dilute to be detected by instruments
   even though a few ppbs of compounds such as mercaptans may produce
   odours even at considerable distances from the plant where they are
   generated. Today methodology allows analysts to detect concentrations as
   low as 10-50 ppt depending upon the mass spectrometric fragmentation
   pattern and the relative abundance of the fragment ion chosen for
   quantitation of the targeted analyte. From the mid eighties several
   methods have appeared, the most complete set From the US EPA, describing
   procedures for sampling and analysis of VOCs in both outdoor and indoor
   air based on the so called `Canister Methodology'. Several applications
   for this Technology can be envisaged where VOCs are present in a variety
   of concentrations, for example urban air monitoring, indoor air
   monitoring, atmospheric emission from landfill sites, refineries and
   industrial plants.},
