Pages = {23-27},
Month = {AUG 1},
Note = {Symposium on Behaviour and Welfare of Extensively Farmed Animals,
   EDINBURGH, SCOTLAND, SEP 03-04, 1994},
Organization = {European Assoc Anim Prod; Commiss European Union; Scottish Ctr Anim
   Welfare Sci},
Abstract = {Most studies of farm animal welfare have been carried out in intensive
   conditions, Considering the welfare of extensively farmed animals
   therefore raises questions of perceptions, processes and priorities,
   There are general perceptions that we are not responsible for the
   welfare of wild animals and that extensive conditions are good for
   welfare compared with intensive conditions. Yet there can be many
   problems for animals in extensive conditions, and these are clearly our
   responsibility, There has been considerable progress in understanding
   problems for welfare, and it is reasonable to expect that the processes
   we know about will operate in all conditions, However, in extrapolating
   from intensive to extensive conditions we must remember the importance
   of exact circumstances, Identification of priorities for the welfare of
   extensively farmed animals requires a systematic effort, which will be
   helped by collaboration with disciplines such as behavioural ecology.},
