Year = {2010},
Pages = {123-125},
Note = {Conference on Modern Research in Psychology: Quantitative vs Qualitative
   Research, Sibiu, ROMANIA, MAY 21-23, 2010},
Abstract = {Adolescence is full of biosomatic and psychological changes and a huge
   adjustment effort. J. Piaget considers ``individual insertion in adult
   society{''} as the fundamental nature of adolescence. The adolescent is
   looking for and feeling strong emotions. He tries to go beyond what
   adults impose on him, mostly just out of desire to disobey them.
   Depending on the way they establish and maintain relationships with
   others, adolescents are satisfied and happy. Some teenagers encounter
   difficulties in establishing new relationships with their peers and
   others destroy relationships because they cannot solve conflicts that
   arise during the relationship. Emotional Intelligence is the one which
   supports human relationships. Adolescents with a high emotional
   intelligence have strong relationships with others and are able to offer
   support to others whenever needed.},
